&lt;p&gt;I genuinely believed I'd be able to do both Step 11 and Step 12 of the &lt;a href="https://cloudresumechallenge.dev/docs/the-challenge/aws/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloud Resume Challenge&lt;/a&gt; this week, but alas, I only got through Step 11. I'm not disappointed, because &lt;strong&gt;time&lt;/strong&gt; is the cost of learning. It would all be in vain if I sped through without understanding what I was actually doing, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Updating the Lambda function
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I updated my Lambda function so it checks for &lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt;, then &lt;code&gt;GET&lt;/code&gt; (using &lt;code&gt;elif&lt;/code&gt;), and returns a &lt;code&gt;405&lt;/code&gt; for anything else. This isn't strictly necessary, because my API Gateway only allows &lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;GET&lt;/code&gt; and rejects other methods earlier. I added it as a safety net in case the function is ever called another way or someone adds a route later (which I doubt will happen), and so I'd have something to test.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;boto3&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;dynamodb&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;boto3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dynamodb"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dynamodb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitor-counter"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;lambda_handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"requestContext"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"http"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;update_item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitors"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;UpdateExpression&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ADD #c :inc"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpressionAttributeNames&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"#c"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpressionAttributeValues&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;":inc"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReturnValues&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"UPDATED_NEW"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Attributes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;elif&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GET"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitors"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Item"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'statusCode'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;405&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'headers'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'body'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dumps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Method not allowed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'statusCode'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'headers'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'body'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dumps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Choosing unit tests and moto
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Step 11 is to add some tests to my Python code. I went with unit tests, because they let me check that each method behaves as it should. They're also fast and free, and they don't touch my real table, so the live counter doesn't change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The article linked in &lt;a href="https://realpython.com/python-testing/#testing-your-code" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Step 11&lt;/a&gt; uses &lt;code&gt;unittest&lt;/code&gt; for its examples, but I used &lt;code&gt;pytest&lt;/code&gt; instead because it needs less code. In &lt;code&gt;unittest&lt;/code&gt;, each test has to live inside a class and use special methods like &lt;code&gt;self.assertEqual()&lt;/code&gt;. In &lt;code&gt;pytest&lt;/code&gt;, a test is just a function that starts with &lt;code&gt;test_&lt;/code&gt;, and I check results with Python's regular &lt;code&gt;assert&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For faking DynamoDB, I compared &lt;code&gt;unittest.mock&lt;/code&gt; (Python's built-in tool) with &lt;code&gt;moto&lt;/code&gt;, and chose &lt;code&gt;moto&lt;/code&gt;. With &lt;code&gt;unittest.mock&lt;/code&gt;, I'd have to write the fake responses myself, so a test could pass even if my code was broken. It would only prove that my code calls the mock the way I expected. &lt;code&gt;moto&lt;/code&gt; simulates DynamoDB in memory, so it's a working fake database. My real code runs against it and does the increment itself, so a result of 6 proves my &lt;code&gt;UpdateExpression&lt;/code&gt; actually works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are the three things I test:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt; increments the count by 1&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;GET&lt;/code&gt; returns the count without changing it&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Any other method returns a &lt;code&gt;405&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  My setup
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I copied my Lambda function into a file on my computer, in a folder called &lt;code&gt;cloud-resume-backend&lt;/code&gt;. Then I created a &lt;code&gt;pytest.ini&lt;/code&gt; file, which is pytest's settings file. pytest reads it before running anything. The only thing I put in it is &lt;code&gt;pythonpath = .&lt;/code&gt;, which tells pytest to look in the root folder for modules. My Lambda function lives there, and without this setting, the tests fail because they can't find it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight ini"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;[pytest]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;pythonpath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;(&lt;code&gt;[pytest]&lt;/code&gt; is the section header that says these settings are for pytest.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next, I created and activated a virtual environment, so the packages stay inside this project and don't get mixed up with the rest of my computer. Note the &lt;code&gt;.fish&lt;/code&gt; in the activate command. That's because I use the &lt;code&gt;fish&lt;/code&gt; shell.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python3 -m venv .venv
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt; .venv/bin/activate.fish
pip install pytest boto3 moto
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then I created a &lt;code&gt;tests&lt;/code&gt; folder with a file called &lt;code&gt;test_lambda.py&lt;/code&gt;. Spoiler alert: this is what took most of my time, because I was learning as I wrote the tests, and my background is more TypeScript than Python. My hope is that the more I do this, the better I'll know the syntax and the steps for writing tests, but that only comes with consistency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The test file
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;importlib&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;boto3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pytest&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;moto&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mock_aws&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@pytest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fixture&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;monkeypatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;monkeypatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"testing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;monkeypatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"testing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;monkeypatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"AWS_DEFAULT_REGION"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ca-central-1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;mock_aws&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;dynamodb&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;boto3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dynamodb"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dynamodb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TableName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitor-counter"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;KeySchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"AttributeName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"KeyType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"HASH"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AttributeDefinitions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"AttributeName"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"AttributeType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"S"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BillingMode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PAY_PER_REQUEST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;put_item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitors"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; 

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_function&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;importlib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;yield&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"requestContext"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"http"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_post_increments_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="n"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lambda_handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; 

   &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"statusCode"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"body"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_get_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="n"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lambda_handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GET"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; 

   &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"statusCode"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"body"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;
   &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitors"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Item"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@pytest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parametrize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"DELETE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PUT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PATCH"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_other_methods_rejected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lambda_module&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lambda_handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; 

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"statusCode"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;405&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visitors"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Item"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"count"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  How the fixture works
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I created &lt;code&gt;lambda_module&lt;/code&gt; and labeled it a &lt;strong&gt;fixture&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;code&gt;@pytest.fixture&lt;/code&gt;. That marks it as a setup helper, not a test. Any test that takes &lt;code&gt;lambda_module&lt;/code&gt; as a parameter receives whatever the fixture hands over. Here's what happens inside it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fake credentials and region&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;monkeypatch&lt;/code&gt; is a pytest tool that makes temporary changes and undoes them when the test finishes. I use it to set fake credentials, because boto3 refuses to talk to AWS unless it has credentials and knows which city to go to (the region). &lt;code&gt;moto&lt;/code&gt; never validates them. The fake details also protect me, because they can't reach my real account.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The pretend AWS&lt;/strong&gt;: Everything inside &lt;code&gt;with mock_aws():&lt;/code&gt;happens in a fake AWS. When the indented block ends, it disappears.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A fake table&lt;/strong&gt;: I create it with the same code I'd use for a real table:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;TableName&lt;/code&gt;: matches the name of my actual table&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;KeySchema&lt;/code&gt;: states rows are found by the column called &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;AttributeDefinitions&lt;/code&gt;: states the &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; holds text, the type is string, &lt;code&gt;S&lt;/code&gt; = string&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;BillingMode&lt;/code&gt;: this is required by AWS when you create a table, so it's set on-demand to match my real table, I don't actually test with it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;One seeded row&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;table.put_item(Item={"id": "visitors", "count": 5})&lt;/code&gt; is my starting point. Every test starts at 5, because the fixture is function-scoped, so it runs fresh for each test and rebuilds the table and the row.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Importing my Lambda&lt;/strong&gt;: I import it inside the pretend AWS, because my Lambda creates its DynamoDB connection when it's first loaded. I want that to happen while the fake AWS and fake credentials exist. If it loaded earlier, the connection could be created before &lt;code&gt;moto&lt;/code&gt; is active or before the fake region is set. The reload is an extra precaution. Python caches imported modules, so &lt;code&gt;reload&lt;/code&gt; re-runs my &lt;code&gt;lambda_function&lt;/code&gt; code from the top for each test. It isn't what makes the data fresh (the function-scoped fixture does that), but it protects me if my Lambda ever keeps state at the top level of the module.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;**yield**&lt;/code&gt;: This pauses the fixture and hands over the &lt;code&gt;lambda_function&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;table&lt;/code&gt; to test. The test runs. Then the fixture resumes, the &lt;code&gt;with&lt;/code&gt; block ends, and the pretend AWS is thrown away.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The three tests
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;POST&lt;/strong&gt;: I check the status is &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and the &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; in the response body went from 5 to 6. I didn't check the table directly, because my Lambda returns the value DynamoDB sends back after the write.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GET&lt;/strong&gt;: I check the status is &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and compare the value with both the response body and the table directly. I imagined a case where the Lambda returns the right value but accidentally writes a new one to the table, so I needed to confirm the value is unchanged.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Other methods&lt;/strong&gt;: I tested that other methods are rejected. Today I learned &lt;code&gt;@pytest.mark.parametrize&lt;/code&gt;, which lets me check for a &lt;code&gt;405&lt;/code&gt; and an unchanged count for &lt;code&gt;DELETE, PUT, and PATCH&lt;/code&gt; without writing three separate tests.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Running them
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I ran &lt;code&gt;pytest -v&lt;/code&gt;, and here's the result. The &lt;code&gt;parametrize&lt;/code&gt; test shows up as three separate results, one for each method.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fufeqtx6ixgnotmoa6kfb.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fufeqtx6ixgnotmoa6kfb.png" alt="pytest -v output showing all tests passing"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As you can see, all I did was write three tests, and they don't take long to write. I took my time because I wanted to understand what I was doing, so I'd know it for next time and not just today. I also learned a lot along the way, from &lt;code&gt;moto&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;fixtures&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;parametrize&lt;/code&gt; and writing the tests themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's all for now. The resume looks the same, but if you haven't seen it, here's the link: &lt;a href="https://lois-aleghe-resume.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Lois resume&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most freelancers find out an invoice is late the same way they find out the milk went off: too late, by accident, when they happen to remember it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is not a better memory. It is a short, boring routine that runs once a month and once a week, so the invoice that is drifting gets noticed while there is still time to do something cheap about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the whole thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why late invoices get expensive
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An invoice that is one day past due is a nudge. An invoice that is 45 days past due is a negotiation. The longer you wait, the more the conversation turns into a request rather than a reminder, and the more likely it is that the person who approved your work has moved on and the person paying you has no context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Catching it early is worth more than any clever wording you can write later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: One monthly pass, 10 minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once a month, open your invoice list and put every unpaid invoice into one of three buckets:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Due in the next 14 days.&lt;/strong&gt; Nothing to do yet, but these are the ones you will be chasing soon.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Overdue by 1 to 7 days.&lt;/strong&gt; These get a short, friendly reminder this week.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Overdue by 8 days or more.&lt;/strong&gt; These need a real message with a date and a next step, not a nudge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is it. You are not writing anything yet, only sorting. The sorting is what stops the surprise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you keep invoices in a spreadsheet, this is a filter on the due date column. If you keep them in your head, this is the moment to stop doing that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Send the pre-due reminder
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cheapest message in the entire collections process is the one you send before the invoice is due.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three working days before the due date, send a two-line note:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Subject: Invoice 1042, due Friday&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hi Sam,&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just a heads up that invoice 1042 for 1,200 is due this Friday, the 14th. The payment details are on the invoice, and let me know if anything is needed from my side to get it through.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks,&lt;br&gt;
Alex&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It reads as helpful, not as a chase, because nothing is late yet. It also gives the client a chance to say "actually, accounts payable needs a purchase order number" while there is still a week to fix it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: A weekly sweep, 15 minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once a week, same day every week, do three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check which invoices crossed the due date since your last sweep.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send the reminder for each one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update the next-action date for each invoice you already chased.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The third item is the one people skip, and it is the one that matters. An invoice with no next-action date is an invoice that will be forgotten until it is awkward.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Write down what was promised
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every time a client says something about payment, write down two things: what they said, and the date you should look again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Sam said it goes out with the next payment run on the 30th" is a note. "Chase on the 2nd if not paid" is an action. You need both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the difference between a client who is quietly drifting and a client who has given you a date. When the date passes, you are not nagging, you are following up on something they said.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Know your escalation line in advance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Decide now, while you are calm, what happens at each stage:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 1 past due:&lt;/strong&gt; a short, friendly reminder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 7:&lt;/strong&gt; a direct reminder with the invoice attached and the payment details repeated.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 14:&lt;/strong&gt; a message that names the next step, such as pausing work or holding delivery.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 30:&lt;/strong&gt; whatever your contract allows, plus a phone call if the amount justifies it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Having the line written down before you need it means the day-14 message gets sent on day 14, instead of on day 26 after three rounds of "just checking in".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The point of all this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these steps is clever. That is the point. A late invoice is usually not a dispute, it is an unattended task, and unattended tasks get handled when there is a routine that surfaces them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ten minutes a month to sort, fifteen minutes a week to sweep, and a written next-action date for every invoice you have chased. That is the whole system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  If you want this in one file
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built a small tool for this exact routine: &lt;strong&gt;Get Paid&lt;/strong&gt;, a single HTML file that runs offline in your browser and keeps track of which invoices you have chased, what was promised, and when to look again. It imports and exports CSV, keeps a backup file, and stores everything locally on your machine. It does not create invoices and it does not send anything for you, you still write and send the messages yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is a one-time purchase of 49.99 on &lt;a href="https://whop.com/the-super-employee-store/get-paid-daily-collections-manager/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Whop&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you would rather build the routine in a spreadsheet, everything above works there too. The routine is the valuable part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every automation tool out there can call a language model. They all added the&lt;br&gt;
node, wired it to the same engine that runs their HTTP nodes, and shipped it in&lt;br&gt;
a week. To be clear, that was the &lt;em&gt;right&lt;/em&gt; call. If your engine was built to&lt;br&gt;
move JSON between APIs, an LLM is just another API. A chatty, expensive one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then people started building agents on top, and everyone found the same thing&lt;br&gt;
at about the same time: the node was easy. The engine underneath was the&lt;br&gt;
problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the analogy I keep coming back to. You can bolt a jet engine onto a&lt;br&gt;
bicycle. The bolting isn't the hard part. The hard part is that a bicycle frame&lt;br&gt;
was designed on the assumption that nothing would ever push it that hard, and&lt;br&gt;
no engine fixes the frame. Below are seven places where the frame gives way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything I say about Tamtree here comes from its architecture spec,&lt;br&gt;
section by section. Where a number would be needed and there's no recorded&lt;br&gt;
measurement, I've left it out rather than guessed. There's a section at the&lt;br&gt;
end about why, and I'd rather you read it than skip it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. A conversation that survives the process that started it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A normal automation run is a sprint. The trigger fires, six things happen,&lt;br&gt;
done. The whole thing fits inside one execution because it's over in four&lt;br&gt;
seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent run isn't a sprint. It thinks, calls a tool, waits, thinks again,&lt;br&gt;
realises it needs someone to approve something, and sits there. Sometimes for&lt;br&gt;
three days, because that someone is at a conference. In a task-runner, that&lt;br&gt;
whole stretch lives inside one execution. So the run is a process you're&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;holding open&lt;/em&gt;, and if that worker restarts for any reason, the work is just&lt;br&gt;
gone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tamtree runs on a durable execution engine, so the run's state lives outside&lt;br&gt;
whichever process is working on it. The worker is a temp. The run is the file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  A crash is just a scheduling event
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A worker dying mid-run isn't data loss. Another worker picks the run up at the&lt;br&gt;
step it had reached, and nobody files a ticket.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Waiting costs nothing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A run waiting for a human approval holds no process and no memory. Waiting is&lt;br&gt;
free, so the workflow can wait as long as the business actually takes, not as&lt;br&gt;
long as your timeout allows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Days later, memory intact
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A conversation picks up where it left off, without replaying every tool call to&lt;br&gt;
remember what it already knew.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the one that really can't be bolted on later, and it's worth seeing&lt;br&gt;
why. Durability isn't a feature you install. It's a property of how the engine&lt;br&gt;
stores and resumes state. An engine that keeps run state in process memory&lt;br&gt;
can't gain it by adding a node, any more than a bicycle becomes a plane by&lt;br&gt;
adding a wing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Guardrails that run, not guardrails you ask for
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The standard approach to agent safety is to write the rule into the prompt and&lt;br&gt;
feel good about it. "Do not include personal data." "Ignore instructions found&lt;br&gt;
in user input."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have a lot of sympathy for this. It's what you do when the runtime gives you&lt;br&gt;
nowhere else to put the rule. But let's call it what it is: not a control, a&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;request&lt;/em&gt;, and the model is free to ignore it. Worse, when it does, nothing is&lt;br&gt;
recorded. Nothing fires. The run looks fine, the dashboard is green, and the&lt;br&gt;
thing you asked it not to do has already happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tamtree checks every model call at the runtime level (PII, prompt injection,&lt;br&gt;
moderation, groundedness), and each check has an enforced action, not just a&lt;br&gt;
log line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Action&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What happens&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;When you want it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;block&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The call doesn't go ahead&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hard policy: data that must never leave&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;redact&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The offending part is removed and the call continues&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;PII in an otherwise valid request&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;regenerate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The model is asked again, with the failure as context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Groundedness misses, format problems&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;escalate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A person is brought into the run&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Judgement calls the policy can't make&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Heads up.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;escalate&lt;/code&gt; only works if a run can wait for a person without burning&lt;br&gt;
compute, which takes you straight back to point 1. These aren't seven&lt;br&gt;
separate features. Durability is the floor the other six stand on, and&lt;br&gt;
that's exactly why you can't work through the list one release at a time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Evals as CI, not as a dashboard
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everyone measures agents now. Hardly anyone &lt;em&gt;blocks a release&lt;/em&gt; on the&lt;br&gt;
measurement, and the gap between those two is where most agent projects&lt;br&gt;
quietly die.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's because an agent regression doesn't look like a crash. A crash is loud,&lt;br&gt;
has a stack trace and pages someone. An agent regression looks like slightly&lt;br&gt;
worse answers: a bit more hedging, a bit less accuracy, a tone that's slightly&lt;br&gt;
off. No alert fires. A customer notices weeks later, in a message that starts&lt;br&gt;
"is it just me, or...".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is the one our industry already worked out for code thirty years ago,&lt;br&gt;
and then somehow didn't apply here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write a suite: test cases with expected &lt;em&gt;behaviour&lt;/em&gt;, not expected strings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Score it, with an LLM judge where the output is prose and plain checks where
it's structure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Block promotion on the score.&lt;/strong&gt; A version that gets worse doesn't reach
production. This is the step everyone skips.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep scoring after release, with feedback and drift alerts, because the
world changes even when your prompt doesn't.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Step three is the whole game. A dashboard tells you what happened; a gate&lt;br&gt;
decides what ships. Only one of them has ever prevented an incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Memory that outlives the conversation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A chat session remembering itself isn't memory. That's a scroll buffer, and&lt;br&gt;
calling it memory is like calling a receipt an accounting system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Memory is your platform knowing that &lt;em&gt;this particular person&lt;/em&gt; asked about&lt;br&gt;
their invoice last month, in a different conversation, on a different channel,&lt;br&gt;
to a different agent. That needs an identity per end user with long-term&lt;br&gt;
storage attached, which is a data-model decision. Data-model decisions get made&lt;br&gt;
early or not at all. No tool has ever managed to grow one halfway through its&lt;br&gt;
life.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Cost you can control, not just watch
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Per step&lt;/strong&gt;: how finely token spend has to be tracked before any of the controls below become possible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the chain, and it only runs one way. Token cost per step feeds a ledger.&lt;br&gt;
The ledger feeds budgets. Budgets feed a kill-switch and model routing. Each&lt;br&gt;
stage depends completely on the one before, and the whole thing starts with&lt;br&gt;
tracking cost per step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why a tool that reports spend per workflow &lt;em&gt;run&lt;/em&gt; can never build the&lt;br&gt;
rest. By the time the run has finished and the number exists, the decision&lt;br&gt;
that number was meant to inform has already been made. By you, looking back,&lt;br&gt;
at the end of the month, in a spreadsheet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6vkrs6rdvp28kcnnv7jv.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6vkrs6rdvp28kcnnv7jv.png" alt="Five ledger rows for one run: two steps succeeded, the third failed, the third appears again as a successful retry, and a fourth step succeeded." width="800" height="378"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One run, five rows. The failed attempt isn't overwritten by the retry. It stays&lt;br&gt;
in the ledger with its own duration and its own cost, because "what did this&lt;br&gt;
actually cost us?" and "why did it take thirty seconds?" are really the same&lt;br&gt;
question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. Two building blocks, one engine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most tools give you a flowchart. Some give you an agent. Tamtree's view is that&lt;br&gt;
all the interesting work happens when you &lt;em&gt;combine&lt;/em&gt; the two. So they have to be&lt;br&gt;
building blocks on the same engine, not two products sharing a logo and a&lt;br&gt;
billing page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pipeline&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Crew&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Control flow&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fixed, author-defined&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The lead agent decides each turn&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Best for&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Repeatable procedures you can audit: sync, ETL, billing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Open-ended work that adapts: research, triage, support&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cost and latency&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low, predictable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Higher, variable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Combining&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A pipeline node can &lt;em&gt;be&lt;/em&gt; a crew&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A crew can call a pipeline as a tool&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last row is the whole point of the table. &lt;strong&gt;Crew as a node&lt;/strong&gt; and&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;pipeline as a tool&lt;/strong&gt; let you put predictability exactly where you can afford&lt;br&gt;
it and autonomy exactly where you need it, inside one workflow. You don't have&lt;br&gt;
to choose once, for everything, forever, on day one, when you understood the&lt;br&gt;
problem least.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Aside.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's also why these can't be two products. The moment a crew calls a&lt;br&gt;
pipeline as a tool, the two share a run, a ledger, a guardrail policy and a&lt;br&gt;
set of credentials. Split the products and you split all four, then have to&lt;br&gt;
reconcile them. That's a worse job than building the engine properly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  7. Using the connector ecosystem instead of rebuilding it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Connector count is a race a newcomer loses by definition, and not narrowly. So&lt;br&gt;
Tamtree doesn't enter it. It's MCP-native: it uses any MCP server as tools and&lt;br&gt;
offers its own workflows as MCP tools in return. Existing automation platforms&lt;br&gt;
get plugged in &lt;em&gt;as tools&lt;/em&gt; rather than rebuilt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tip.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a strategy decision dressed up as an integration one. Rebuilding four&lt;br&gt;
hundred connectors is years of work, and the prize is &lt;em&gt;catching up&lt;/em&gt;. Calling&lt;br&gt;
them is a week of work, and the prize is the same reach. I know which of&lt;br&gt;
those I'd rather spend a year of engineering on, and it's neither. It's the&lt;br&gt;
engine everything else in this post depends on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'm not claiming
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Watch out.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's no latency, cost or throughput figure for Tamtree anywhere in this&lt;br&gt;
post, and that's on purpose. The product is pre-launch. Any number I gave&lt;br&gt;
today would be a guess dressed up as a measurement, and the only thing worse&lt;br&gt;
than having no benchmark is having one that turns out to be wishful.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Numbers will appear here when they come from recorded runs, and they'll say&lt;br&gt;
which run they came from. Until then, no numbers, and my apologies for that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.tamtree.io/blog/tamtree-workflow-builder/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read how the builder handles day forty&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;First published on the &lt;a href="https://www.tamtree.io/blog/what-automation-tools-cannot-do/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tamtree blog&lt;/a&gt;. Tamtree is an agent-first workflow orchestrator: fixed pipelines and AI agent crews on one crash-proof engine. More at &lt;a href="https://www.tamtree.io/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tamtree.io&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Supabase warns you when a table in the &lt;code&gt;public&lt;/code&gt; schema has Row Level Security (RLS) turned off. You may see it as a Security Advisor warning in the dashboard (the lint is called &lt;code&gt;rls_disabled_in_public&lt;/code&gt;) or as an email about a publicly accessible table. If you got one, treat it as real. Here is what to do, in order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the warning means
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With RLS off, any request that carries your project's anon key can read, and often write, that table through the API. The anon key is in your app's JavaScript, so anyone can get it. The table is open to the internet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Turn RLS on
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the SQL editor:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;alter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;level&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;security&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With RLS on and no policies, nobody except the service role can access the table. Your app will stop reading it until you add policies. That is the safe default.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Add the narrowest policy that works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For data that belongs to a user:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"owner can read"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;policy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"owner can insert"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;your_table&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;insert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add update and delete policies the same way. Avoid &lt;code&gt;using (true)&lt;/code&gt; on anything private.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Check what was exposed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open your API logs and check whether the table was read by unknown clients. If it held personal data, tell your own legal or privacy contact. The duties depend on where your users are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Test it from outside
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Signed out, request the table with only the anon key and confirm you get no private rows. Then test with two accounts: user B must not see user A's rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Look for the rest
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The warning names one table. Check all of them:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tablename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rowsecurity&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pg_tables&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;schemaname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'public'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Also check storage bucket policies and any &lt;code&gt;service_role&lt;/code&gt; key in your front-end code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Free count-only checker for a project you own: &lt;a href="https://proofforge.live/tools/supabase-rls-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://proofforge.live/tools/supabase-rls-checker&lt;/a&gt; . Fix guide: &lt;a href="https://proofforge.live/fix/supabase-rls-disabled" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://proofforge.live/fix/supabase-rls-disabled&lt;/a&gt; . Policy error you may hit next: &lt;a href="https://proofforge.live/errors/new-row-violates-row-level-security-policy" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://proofforge.live/errors/new-row-violates-row-level-security-policy&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Crypto Twitter loves one chart: retail traders are heavily long while the "top traders" are short, so a squeeze must be coming. Exchanges publish the numbers behind that chart for free, so I pulled 100 hours of them for 20 coins on three exchanges and checked how often it happens and what follows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short version: it happens &lt;strong&gt;80% of the time&lt;/strong&gt;. It is the default, not an event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where the numbers come from
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Binance and Bybit refuse requests from US cloud servers (HTTP 451 and 403), so a scheduled scraper can't rely on them. Three big venues answer every time, with no key:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OKX&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://www.okx.com/api/v5/rubik/stat/contracts/long-short-account-ratio-contract?instId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;USDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;SWAP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1H&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://www.okx.com/api/v5/rubik/stat/contracts/long-short-account-ratio-contract-top-trader?instId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;USDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;SWAP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1H&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://www.okx.com/api/v5/rubik/stat/contracts/long-short-position-ratio-contract-top-trader?instId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;USDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;SWAP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1H&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bitget&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://api.bitget.com/api/v2/mix/market/long-short?symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTCUSDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1h&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://api.bitget.com/api/v2/mix/market/account-long-short?symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTCUSDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1h&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://api.bitget.com/api/v2/mix/market/position-long-short?symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTCUSDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1h&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gate&lt;/strong&gt; (one call, and it adds open interest, liquidations and mark price)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://api.gateio.ws/api/v4/futures/usdt/contract_stats?contract&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BTC_USDT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each returns a &lt;strong&gt;ratio&lt;/strong&gt; (long accounts ÷ short accounts). To turn it into "% long", use &lt;code&gt;ratio / (1 + ratio)&lt;/code&gt;. A ratio of 2.84 means 74% of accounts are long.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.okx.com/api/v5/rubik/stat/contracts/long-short-account-ratio-contract?instId=BTC-USDT-SWAP&amp;amp;period=1H'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${(ratio / (1 + +ratio) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of accounts long`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two gotchas:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The newest bucket is still in progress.&lt;/strong&gt; Its taker volume is partial (I saw 0 buys on SOL for the current hour). Use the last &lt;em&gt;completed&lt;/em&gt; period.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;"Top trader" means something different on each exchange.&lt;/strong&gt; Compare direction and change over time, not the level across venues.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How often the crowd and top traders disagree
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I labelled each reading "crowd long, top traders short" when all accounts were net long (ratio &amp;gt; 1) but top traders' &lt;strong&gt;positions&lt;/strong&gt; were net short (ratio &amp;lt; 1).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Exchange&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Readings&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Crowd long, top short&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Aligned&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OKX&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,881&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;83%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;17%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Gate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,900&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;82%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bitget&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;116&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;75%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On OKX and Gate, retail is long and the big accounts lean short almost all the time. The averages explain why: across 20 coins, &lt;strong&gt;67% of OKX accounts and 64% of Gate accounts are long&lt;/strong&gt;, while top traders' positions average &lt;strong&gt;47% long&lt;/strong&gt;. Small accounts skew long (people buy coins they like, often with leverage). Large accounts hedge, run basis trades and make markets, so their book leans short even when they are not bearish.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bitget looks different only because it defines its groups differently. Its "all accounts" number averages 82% long.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Does it predict anything?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gate publishes the mark price with each reading, so I checked the &lt;strong&gt;next 4 hours&lt;/strong&gt; for every Gate reading:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Condition&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Readings&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Avg 4h return&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Up %&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;All readings&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,824&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+0.04%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;48%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Crowd long, top short&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,500&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+0.04%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;48%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Aligned&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;321&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+0.06%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Crowd 70%+ long&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;172&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;−0.17%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;41%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The famous divergence predicts nothing: same returns as everything else. The only thing that leaned anywhere was the extreme, &lt;strong&gt;70%+ of accounts long&lt;/strong&gt;, which was followed by a slightly negative 4 hours. That is 100 hours of one week's market, 172 overlapping readings, so treat it as "worth watching", not a strategy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd watch instead
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Extremes and changes, not the state.&lt;/strong&gt; 80% of retail long is normal; a jump from 65% to 78% in a day is information.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The same coin across exchanges.&lt;/strong&gt; If OKX, Gate and Bitget all hit their own highs at once, that is a broader crowding than any one venue.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Positions, not accounts.&lt;/strong&gt; Top-trader position ratios move less and mean more than account counts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Data and a ready-made scraper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 3,915 hourly readings (20 coins × 3 exchanges, plus open interest and liquidations from Gate) are free on Kaggle: &lt;a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/kenmutisya/crypto-long-short-ratio-sample" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Crypto Long/Short Ratio: 20 Coins, 3 Exchanges&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To collect your own on a schedule, the scraper that maps all three exchanges onto one schema is on Apify as &lt;a href="https://apify.com/scrapemint/crypto-long-short-ratio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Crypto Long/Short Ratio&lt;/a&gt;. Snapshot mode gives the latest completed hour; history mode backfills up to 100 points.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do you trade positioning data, or is it noise to you?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A batting line tells you what happened. Statcast tells you what &lt;em&gt;should&lt;/em&gt; have happened, based on how hard and at what angle each ball was hit. The gap between the two, &lt;strong&gt;wOBA minus xwOBA&lt;/strong&gt;, is the best quick read on which hitters ran hot or cold on luck, and which ones are likely to swing back next season.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MLB publishes both, for free, in two places. Here is how to join them, and what the 2026 season looks like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Source 1: the MLB Stats API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The official API behind MLB.com, no key:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://statsapi.mlb.com/api/v1/stats?stats&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;season&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;group&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;season&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sportId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;playerPool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each split has &lt;code&gt;player.id&lt;/code&gt; (the MLBAM id), &lt;code&gt;team&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;position&lt;/code&gt; and a &lt;code&gt;stat&lt;/code&gt; object with everything from &lt;code&gt;plateAppearances&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;ops&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;babip&lt;/code&gt;. Use &lt;code&gt;group=pitching&lt;/code&gt; for pitchers and &lt;code&gt;playerPool=qualified&lt;/code&gt; for title qualifiers only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Source 2: Baseball Savant CSVs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Savant's leaderboards export CSV with the same player id:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/leaderboard/expected_statistics?type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;position&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;team&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;GET https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/leaderboard/statcast?type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;position&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;team&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first has &lt;code&gt;ba&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;est_ba&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;slg&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;est_slg&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;woba&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;est_woba&lt;/code&gt; (and &lt;code&gt;era&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;xera&lt;/code&gt; for pitchers). The second has exit velocity, launch angle, hard-hit % and barrels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two gotchas cost me time:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The CSV starts with a &lt;strong&gt;byte-order mark&lt;/strong&gt;, so your first column header is &lt;code&gt;﻿last_name, first_name&lt;/code&gt;, not &lt;code&gt;last_name, first_name&lt;/code&gt;. Strip it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The column &lt;code&gt;est_woba_minus_woba_diff&lt;/code&gt; is actually &lt;strong&gt;wOBA minus xwOBA&lt;/strong&gt;, the opposite of what its name says. Check it against two players before you trust the sign.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The join
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stats&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://statsapi.mlb.com/api/v1/stats?stats=season&amp;amp;group=hitting&amp;amp;season=2026&amp;amp;sportId=1&amp;amp;playerPool=all&amp;amp;limit=3000'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csv&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/leaderboard/expected_statistics?type=batter&amp;amp;year=2026&amp;amp;position=&amp;amp;team=&amp;amp;min=1&amp;amp;csv=true'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;﻿&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

