&lt;p&gt;If you'd rather not juggle six endpoints and two roster shapes, I put the whole thing on Apify as &lt;a href="https://apify.com/scrapemint/espn-sports-data" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ESPN Sports Data&lt;/a&gt;. One input picks scores (with the betting line), standings, injuries, rosters, news or teams for any of 25+ leagues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What would you build on top of it?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fybox7inb3l41fuk731i1.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fybox7inb3l41fuk731i1.png" alt="Minellion Games — Daily &amp;amp; Unlimited Minecraft Reverse-Trivia" width="800" height="420"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reverse-trivia browser games—where common answers barely score and obscure correct answers win big—took off with &lt;strong&gt;Krillion&lt;/strong&gt; and quickly inspired Minecraft-focused daily games like &lt;strong&gt;Minellion&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As a web developer who loves building lightweight, zero-framework browser games, I built &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://minelliongames.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Minellion Games&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; as an independent fan-made browser implementation focused on fast mobile typing, zero external iframes, unlimited category practice, and honest 1.21+ rarity scoring. &lt;em&gt;(Note: Minellion Games is an independent fan project—not an official Minecraft product, and not affiliated with Mojang AB, Microsoft, or Hydro's original &lt;code&gt;minellion.io&lt;/code&gt;.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a look at the key frontend engineering problems I solved while building it, followed by a breakdown of the best browser games similar to Minellion and Krillion if you are looking for daily trivia alternatives.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Zero External Iframes: Fixing Mobile Keyboards &amp;amp; School Chromebook Access
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most browser game aggregators wrap external domains inside an &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;iframe&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. On paper that is quick to ship, but in practice it causes three recurring UX failures for text-input trivia games:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mobile virtual keyboard clipping:&lt;/strong&gt; On iOS Safari and Android Chrome, an input inside a cross-origin iframe often scrolls out of view or fails to trigger clean focus transitions when the software keyboard opens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;School &amp;amp; office network blocks:&lt;/strong&gt; Campus filters frequently block cross-origin game frames even when the host domain is allowed, leaving players staring at an empty gray box.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audio &amp;amp; mute friction:&lt;/strong&gt; Players in quiet classrooms or offices cannot easily mute a Web Audio context trapped in another origin.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On &lt;code&gt;minelliongames.com&lt;/code&gt;, the entire quarry console, autocomplete dropdown, strata depth meter, and 1.21+ JSON lexicon run natively in first-party HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript. There is no app download, no account signup wall, and no iframe boundary—so it runs directly in Chrome, Safari, Firefox, and Edge across Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android, iPads, and school Chromebooks (with a dedicated silent-friendly &lt;a href="https://minelliongames.com/unblocked" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Minellion Unblocked&lt;/a&gt; entry point).&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Handling 25-Second Typing Pressure: Shorthand Aliases &amp;amp; Fuzzy Matching
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Asking a mobile player to type &lt;code&gt;Waxed Weathered Cut Copper Stairs&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;Enchanted Golden Apple&lt;/code&gt; under a 25-second countdown is brutal if your validator only checks exact strings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To keep the challenge about Minecraft knowledge rather than thumb-typing precision, I implemented a three-layer matcher:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canonical shorthand map:&lt;/strong&gt; Common community abbreviations resolve straight to their canonical 1.21+ entity (&lt;code&gt;wither skelly&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;Wither Skeleton&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gap&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;gapple&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;Golden Apple&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;egap&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;Enchanted Golden Apple&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;e-pearl&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;Ender Pearl&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;totem&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;Totem of Undying&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;cmd block&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;Command Block&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;1–2 character typo tolerance:&lt;/strong&gt; Minor spelling slips on longer biome, enchantment, or Trial Chamber names match cleanly against the active prompt's valid set.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Live retry while the clock runs:&lt;/strong&gt; If an entry isn't recognized or doesn't fit the prompt category, the game gives immediate inline feedback so the player can edit and resubmit before time expires—while enforcing a one-use-per-entity rule across the 7-prompt shaft.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Solving the Cold-Start Problem in Reverse-Trivia Rarity Scoring
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a reverse-trivia digging game, rarer valid answers push your drill deeper from &lt;strong&gt;Surface Grass (&lt;code&gt;Y+64&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt; down &lt;strong&gt;128 blocks to Bedrock (&lt;code&gt;Y-64&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt; and beyond:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Common (&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;25%&lt;/code&gt; pick share):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;0–6&lt;/code&gt; blocks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Uncommon (&lt;code&gt;12%–25%&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;7–13&lt;/code&gt; blocks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rare (&lt;code&gt;5%–12%&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;14–19&lt;/code&gt; blocks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Epic (&lt;code&gt;1%–5%&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;20–25&lt;/code&gt; blocks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One in a Minellion (&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;1%&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;26–30&lt;/code&gt; blocks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tricky engineering edge case happens right after the &lt;code&gt;00:00 UTC&lt;/code&gt; daily reset when only a handful of people have played a prompt. If player #1 types &lt;code&gt;Creeper&lt;/code&gt; and you calculate rarity naively from &lt;code&gt;n = 1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Creeper&lt;/code&gt; looks like 100%, while player #2's &lt;code&gt;Zombie&lt;/code&gt; looks rare.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To avoid noisy early-day scores, the console pairs a &lt;strong&gt;calibrated Baseline rarity benchmark&lt;/strong&gt; (used when a daily prompt has fewer than 5 verified community picks) with &lt;strong&gt;Live player pick rate telemetry&lt;/strong&gt; once 5+ real players submit answers on that prompt. Players can also inspect the full calibrated dataset of 400+ Minecraft 1.21+ entries anytime in the searchable &lt;a href="https://minelliongames.com/rarity-guide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Minellion Rarity Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Client-Side Canvas Share Cards &amp;amp; Multiple Game Modes in One Script
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two features that noticeably boost replayability in daily puzzle games are &lt;strong&gt;visual sharing&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;zero-cooldown practice&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Client-side &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;canvas&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; PNG export:&lt;/strong&gt; After prompt #7, the summary screen plots both a &lt;strong&gt;Depth Distribution&lt;/strong&gt; normal curve and a 7-shaft &lt;strong&gt;Mine Shaft Log&lt;/strong&gt;, then renders a crisp &lt;code&gt;1200×675&lt;/code&gt; shareable PNG card directly in the browser via &lt;code&gt;HTMLCanvasElement.toBlob()&lt;/code&gt;—zero server-side image rendering costs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mode switching without separate builds:&lt;/strong&gt; The same lightweight console powers the shared 7-prompt Daily Dig, &lt;a href="https://minelliongames.com/unlimited" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Minellion Unlimited&lt;/a&gt; (6 category packs across Mobs, Blocks, Items, Biomes, Enchantments, and Structures with &lt;code&gt;25s&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;20s Blitz&lt;/code&gt;, and untimed &lt;code&gt;Zen&lt;/code&gt; timers), and &lt;a href="https://minelliongames.com/creeper-party" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Creeper Party&lt;/a&gt; (local Pass &amp;amp; Play hot-potato multiplayer for 2–12 friends on one screen with duplicate-answer lockout).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




